{"connection_id": "BC-df4c3ad0eabf8419a60d", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.10.4", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "spiritual drink from the rock interpreted as Christ", "note": "Paul identifies Israel's water-giving rock with Christ. This Christian reading brings the commentary's rock-and-water image into a specific typological tradition rather than treating it as a generic well motif."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6323824cea3c44409e09", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.42", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "perishable body raised imperishable", "note": "The resurrection is sown perishable and raised imperishable, placing the commentary's language of permanence in a bodily Christian eschatological setting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6d69cbb698d612022e19", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.13", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fire tests each one's work", "note": "The commentary's metal foam separates from useful material, and its later image distinguishes what vanishes from what remains; Paul says fire tests the quality of each person's work, giving that separation an explicit judgment setting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-85f01efa5e5ef40c1316", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.14", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "work that remains receives reward", "note": "The commentary says the hereafter is more abiding and that foam goes while what benefits remains; Paul says the builder whose work remains receives a reward, linking endurance to evaluated work."}
{"connection_id": "BC-30cd1d593b250f480662", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.15", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "burned work means loss", "note": "The commentary casts the residue as what goes and is not counted; Paul says burned work brings loss, the contrary outcome to work that survives the fire."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1f6dfa6a5b7d6b0b66b2", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.15", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "work burned and its maker suffers loss", "note": "In the fire-test of works, what burns is lost. Alongside the commentary's metal-smelting foam, it supplies a Christian judgment image where material does not endure the furnace."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bc0bc9b5b3b7ea4f03e7", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.4.5", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hidden motives brought to light", "note": "The commentary says God knows what is hidden; Paul says the Lord will bring hidden things to light and disclose heart's purposes, specifying hidden human motives as the object of that knowledge."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fcf2efca85409584bf77", "ref": "SBLGNT:1John.2.17", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "world passes; God's will remains", "note": "The commentary contrasts vanishing foam with the more abiding hereafter; 1 John says the world and its desire pass away while the one doing God's will remains forever, giving the contrast an ethical criterion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-92fbb91167460d12c969", "ref": "SBLGNT:1John.2.17", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "world passes away; God-doer remains forever", "note": "The world and its desire pass away while the one doing God's will remains forever. It gives a concise ethical form to the commentary's passing-versus-abiding contrast."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7683196b1f489abddd15", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.24", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "all human glory like grass", "note": "The commentary's dried pasture becomes residue that disperses; Peter quotes Isaiah's grass and flower image for all flesh and human glory, extending the fading-vegetation picture to human life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fdb6980737f3d1d4186f", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.24", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "grass withers and flower falls", "note": "Peter applies the grass-and-flower image to mortal flesh; it gives a close Christian counterpart to the commentary's pasture becoming transient debris."}
{"connection_id": "BC-79916939ae0aa64e53e7", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.25", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "word of the Lord remains forever", "note": "The commentary pairs beneficial reminder with an abiding hereafter; Peter quotes the grass that withers and then says the Lord's word remains forever, applying Isaiah's transience contrast to the proclaimed good news."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cb56c248b83988181a43", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.25", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "word remains forever", "note": "The verse makes the preached word enduring after the preceding grass has withered, a direct analogue for the reminder whose benefit outlasts what goes away."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1f83d796c0ab46dd5c61", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.4", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "imperishable heavenly inheritance", "note": "The commentary says the hereafter is better and more abiding; 1 Peter describes an inheritance kept in heaven as imperishable, undefiled, and unfading, specifying what such permanence means."}
{"connection_id": "BC-88a30274d5d5d56c42fa", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.7", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "faith tested as gold by fire", "note": "The commentary invokes metal melted for useful things and contrasts transient residue with lasting good; Peter compares tested faith to gold refined by fire and locates its result at Jesus' appearing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ecba6bcd299576145d32", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.7", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "perishable gold tested by fire and proven faith", "note": "Peter calls even fire-tested gold perishable while faith is proved through trial, a carefully different but relevant interpretation of the commentary's molten-metal image and its question of what endures."}
{"connection_id": "BC-642095e0acc48782d60d", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.3.20", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "few saved through flood water", "note": "Peter's flood reference makes water the medium through which a few are preserved rather than merely a current that carries refuse away. That reversal gives the commentary's flood image a distinct Christian eschatological reading."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9c606a90c5eaea018f8b", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.3.21", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "flood water interpreted through baptism and a good conscience", "note": "The next verse interprets the flood-water figure as baptism, expressly denying a merely bodily washing and locating its benefit in an appeal to God through resurrection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c7cdf473396a57bce4fa", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.4.8", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "piety useful now and for the coming life", "note": "Piety is called beneficial and is said to promise life both now and in the future. The verse tightly pairs the commentary's benefit vocabulary with its final-life horizon."}
{"connection_id": "BC-48c5e4371a3eb21f658a", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.4.18", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seen is transient; unseen is eternal", "note": "The commentary pairs God's knowledge of what is public and hidden with a more abiding hereafter; Paul contrasts visible transient things with unseen eternal things, bringing hiddenness and permanence together."}

{"connection_id": "BC-f636887e54f83cce8709", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.4.18", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "visible temporary and invisible eternal", "note": "Paul explicitly contrasts the temporary seen realm with the eternal unseen one, a close Pauline formulation of the commentary's final better-and-more-enduring horizon."}
{"connection_id": "BC-845f8403d48c5bec7a50", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.5.1", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "destroyed earthly dwelling and eternal heavenly dwelling", "note": "Paul contrasts the destructible earthly tent with an eternal dwelling from God. It gives the commentary's better, more enduring hereafter a concrete dwelling-place form."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a210c9da8339929b0557", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Pet.2.17", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "waterless springs and storm-driven mists as false teachers", "note": "False teachers are waterless springs and mists driven by a storm. The deliberately unhelpful water image gives a sharp inverse to the commentary's reminder that must benefit and water that truly remains."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ad4a2ac4ee402cf211ee", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.3.16", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Scripture is profitable for instruction", "note": "The commentary asks whether a reminder benefits and defines benefit by help toward good; Paul describes Scripture as useful for teaching, reproof, correction, and training in righteousness, a direct Christian account of beneficial instruction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4e7557d116fbbc62c6fa", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.3.16", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Scripture useful for teaching, correction, and training", "note": "Scripture is explicitly useful for teaching and correction, a precise Christian formulation of admonition whose value lies in actually helping its recipient."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f492479013a6590d96e8", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.3.17", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "equipped for every good work", "note": "The commentary links benefit to reaching good and the hereafter to what is better; Paul says Scripture equips the person of God for every good work, identifying the practical result of useful instruction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a266c633d7a1269df69d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.5.26", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "washing of water with the word", "note": "Ephesians explicitly joins cleansing water to the word, a close Christian analogue to the commentary's clear well and reminder that does good."}
{"connection_id": "BC-73e77e7dd57c38782492", "ref": "SBLGNT:Gal.6.8", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sowing to decay or to eternal life", "note": "Paul contrasts a harvest of corruption from flesh with eternal life from the Spirit. The agricultural separation adds a Christian ethical form to the commentary's perishing residue and abiding good."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e71338bb6178414d9dbc", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.10.34", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "better and abiding possession", "note": "The commentary describes the hereafter as better and more abiding; Hebrews says its addressees know they possess a better and abiding possession, a close formulation of the same contrast in a persecution context."}
{"connection_id": "BC-14f98a0028872c34ac09", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.27", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "shaken things removed; unshaken things remain", "note": "The commentary's foam goes while water remains and calls the hereafter more abiding; Hebrews says what can be shaken is removed so that what cannot be shaken remains, making permanence the outcome of divine judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f5c88433a523f40d4626", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.27", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "shaken things removed so unshakable things remain", "note": "Hebrews reads cosmic shaking as removal of what can be shaken so that what cannot be shaken may remain, an eschatological counterpart to separating passing surface matter from what stays."}
{"connection_id": "BC-273ac6fb4d15861ce07f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.13.14", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "no lasting city here and a future city sought", "note": "Hebrews explicitly denies a lasting city in the present and directs attention to the one to come, closely matching the commentary's contrast between what goes and the more abiding final reality."}
{"connection_id": "BC-01436dce9a441714a915", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.4.2", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "message heard but not benefiting its hearers", "note": "Hebrews says that a proclaimed message did not benefit some hearers because it was not joined to faith. It is an unusually exact Christian counterpoint to the commentary's conditional, benefit-bearing reminder."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5cbac5b1852fe51398f3", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.6.7", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rain yields a useful crop", "note": "The commentary defines benefit as what helps attain good and sets retained water against useless residue; Hebrews pictures ground drinking repeated rain and producing a useful crop, explicitly joining water with benefit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9fedf0e5d732a4654e52", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.6.7", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "land drinks rain and yields useful vegetation", "note": "Land that drinks repeated rain and bears a crop useful to its cultivators receives blessing. Its exact useful-water logic closely mirrors the commentary's water that remains and benefits people."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bf463507735d98f806a0", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.6.8", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "thorns rejected and burned", "note": "The commentary's dried plant matter becomes flood refuse that goes away; Hebrews contrasts useful crop with land bearing thorns and thistles, rejected and ending in fire, giving the unproductive growth an ethical and judgmental outcome."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0daa518180617018e783", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.6.8", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "thorns prove worthless and end in burning", "note": "The same field image sets useful rain-fed growth against worthless thorns whose end is burning, adding a Christian antithesis to the commentary's residue versus what is discarded."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7bae40d3a967d99e12d6", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.11", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "flower fades and rich man withers", "note": "The commentary calls the plant residue dried and passing; James likens the rich person to a flower that withers under scorching heat, applying the fading-plant image to worldly prosperity."}
{"connection_id": "BC-317b2d4b2c3b5d515932", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.11", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "scorched grass and fallen flower", "note": "James describes sun-scorched grass, a fallen flower, and fading beauty; it closely echoes the commentary's pasture that becomes dry, transient matter."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1670327abcdb922e2e9d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.6", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wave driven and tossed by wind", "note": "The doubter is a sea-wave driven by wind, a specific instability image that helps mark why the commentary values what settles, remains, and can benefit."}

{"connection_id": "BC-97aa5db646887902cf35", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.3.11", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "one spring cannot yield fresh and bitter water", "note": "The commentary's source gloss describes cleaning a well until clear water appears, and it presents reminder as speech that may benefit; James uses spring-water quality to test speech, asking whether one opening can yield both fresh and bitter water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a74750e6a51982c74293", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.3.11", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "one spring cannot issue fresh and bitter water", "note": "James uses a spring's water quality to test speech, giving the commentary's clear well an ethical and verbal extension rather than a merely physical one."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7065228890fcfd9f6606", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.4.14", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water springing up to eternal life", "note": "The commentary connects water held in rock hollows with the hereafter's greater permanence; Jesus says the water he gives becomes a spring welling up to eternal life, joining water's benefit to enduring life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4bc12a56a0c7f5cff805", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.4.14", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water becomes a spring into eternal life", "note": "Jesus' water becomes an inner spring that wells into eternal life, bringing together well water, benefit, and the enduring final life named in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-95abc8faea74595bae28", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.6.27", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "perishing food and food enduring to eternal life", "note": "Jesus contrasts food that perishes with food enduring to eternal life, directly joining utility, perishing, and the permanent life foregrounded by the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dc3971f7f48f83372d5b", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.7.38", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "flowing rivers of living water", "note": "The believer is promised flowing living water; it develops the commentary's life-bearing abundance beyond water merely trapped in a well or hollow."}
{"connection_id": "BC-918e90e76121d2c7c25d", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.7.39", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "living-water image explained as the Spirit", "note": "The narrator identifies the preceding rivers of living water with the Spirit, giving an internal Christian interpretation that prevents the water image from being read as only physical supply."}
{"connection_id": "BC-baaebb7354f97347d27c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jude.1.12", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "waterless clouds carried by winds and fruitless uprooted trees", "note": "Jude's false teachers promise water but carry none and are wind-driven, fruitless trees. This combines empty water, dispersal, and failed vegetation against the commentary's useful retained water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5000c942aaa324375672", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.3.17", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wheat gathered; chaff burned", "note": "The commentary's غثاء is plant residue that disperses and counts for nothing; John gathers wheat into the barn and burns the chaff, making agricultural separation a judgment image of what is kept and what is discarded."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4cfec57b6f1842feed17", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.6.48", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "digging deep to rock; flood cannot shake the house", "note": "The hearer who acts digs deep, founds on rock, and withstands a flood. This is a particularly close meeting of useful admonition, a worked depth in rock, and endurance under rushing water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c36354eb92197b9a9111", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.8.15", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "word held fast and bearing fruit", "note": "The commentary makes reminder beneficial only where it helps toward good and compares it to water held in hollows; Luke's good soil hears the word, holds it fast, and bears fruit with patience, giving reception and retention an ethical outcome."}
{"connection_id": "BC-27820c6b218566ede352", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.13.31", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "passing heaven and earth; imperishable words", "note": "Jesus contrasts the passing of heaven and earth with words that will not pass, a direct word-level counterpart to the commentary's contrast between what goes away and what is more lasting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-166067f8c62825b2e5e0", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.19", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "earthly treasure is destroyed or stolen", "note": "The commentary's foam is what goes while beneficial value remains; Jesus names moth, rust, and thieves as agents that remove earthly treasure, supplying concrete forms of worldly loss against the heavenly treasure in the next verse."}
